{"fileVersion":"1.0","metadata":{"title":"Twilite Graph Primitives","description":"A topic graph explaining Twilite's graph-native primitives, their contracts, and how semantic relationships compose them.","graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives","version":"1.1.0","kind":"documentation","created":"2026-09-17T17:23:00.000Z","modified":"2026-09-17T18:07:52.110Z","author":"","tags":["twilite","graph-primitives","topic","topic-template-v0.3","graph-native"],"preferredViewer":"https://twilite.zone"},"nodes":[{"id":"gp-decl","type":"declaration","label":"Twilite Graph Primitives","position":{"x":-900,"y":-1000},"width":400,"height":260,"ports":[{"id":"default-view","label":"default view","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":165},{"id":"summary-view","label":"summary view","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":180},{"id":"icon-view","label":"icon view","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":195},{"id":"glyph","label":"glyph","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"landing-surface","label":"landing surface","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":0}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives","name":"Twilite Graph Primitives","version":"1.1.0","description":"Twilite's core graph-native primitives, their contracts, and the semantic relationships that compose them."},"intent":{"kind":"documentation","scope":"public"},"dependencies":{"nodeTypes":["declaration","view","content","glyph","bridge","topic","topic-concept","topic-claim","topic-mechanism","topic-perspective","topic-source","topic-synthesis"],"portContracts":["core"],"skills":["topic-template"]},"authority":{"mutation":{"allowCreate":true,"allowUpdate":true,"allowDelete":true,"appendOnly":false},"actors":{"humans":true,"agents":true,"tools":true}},"settings":{"layout":{"mode":"manual"}},"declaration":{"kind":"graph","targetMode":"artifact","interfaceContract":{"version":1,"implicitRootPort":true},"defaultSurfaceId":"default-view","surfaces":[{"id":"default-view","kind":"view","label":"Detail","viewNodeId":"gp-detail"},{"id":"summary-view","kind":"view","label":"Summary","viewNodeId":"gp-summary"},{"id":"icon-view","kind":"view","label":"Icon","viewNodeId":"gp-icon"},{"id":"glyph","kind":"view","label":"Glyph","viewNodeId":"gp-glyph"},{"id":"landing-surface","kind":"view","label":"Landing","viewNodeId":"gp-land"}]},"document":{"url":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/topics/twilite-graph-primitives/root.node"}}},{"id":"gp-detail","type":"view","label":"Detail","position":{"x":-1700,"y":-520},"width":650,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":210}],"handles":[{"id":"root","portId":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"},"content":{"kind":"markdown","value":"# Twilite Graph Primitives\n\nTwilite builds graph-backed artifacts from a deliberately small structural vocabulary: **Declaration, Port, View, Content, Glyph, Portal, and Bridge**.\n\nEach primitive owns one narrow responsibility. The expressive power comes from explicit semantic relationships between them. A View is not inherently a summary or icon. A Port is not a screen coordinate. A Bridge is not an invisible import. Meaning lives in authored contracts, so presentation, navigation, authority, storage, and geometry can evolve without collapsing into one another."}}},{"id":"gp-summary","type":"view","label":"Summary","position":{"x":-1700,"y":-900},"width":520,"height":220,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":210}],"handles":[{"id":"root","portId":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":0}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"},"content":{"kind":"markdown","value":"## Graph primitives\n\n**Declaration · Port · View · Content · Glyph · Portal · Bridge**\n\nSmall structural roles. Explicit semantic endpoints. Relationships compose them into rich graph-backed artifacts."}}},{"id":"gp-icon","type":"view","label":"Icon","position":{"x":-1450,"y":-1320},"width":320,"height":220,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":210}],"handles":[{"id":"root","portId":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":90}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"},"content":{"kind":"svg","value":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 320 220' role='img' aria-label='Graph primitives'><rect width='320' height='220' rx='28' fill='#0f172a'/><circle cx='160' cy='110' r='30' fill='#60a5fa'/><circle cx='80' cy='60' r='14' fill='#34d399'/><circle cx='160' cy='45' r='14' fill='#f59e0b'/><circle cx='240' cy='60' r='14' fill='#a78bfa'/><circle cx='80' cy='160' r='14' fill='#fb7185'/><circle cx='160' cy='175' r='14' fill='#22d3ee'/><circle cx='240' cy='160' r='14' fill='#facc15'/><path d='M96 70L135 95M160 62V80M224 70L185 95M96 150L135 125M160 158V140M224 150L185 125' stroke='#e2e8f0' stroke-width='5'/><text x='160' y='115' text-anchor='middle' fill='#0f172a' font-family='system-ui' font-size='12' font-weight='900'>PRIMITIVES</text></svg>"}}},{"id":"gp-glyph","type":"glyph","label":"Primitives Glyph","position":{"x":-780,"y":-1320},"width":180,"height":150,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":210}],"handles":[{"id":"root","portId":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":90}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"},"glyph":{"kind":"icon","name":"Hub"}}},{"id":"gp-land","type":"content","label":"Landing","position":{"x":360,"y":-620},"width":620,"height":260,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":210}],"handles":[{"id":"root","portId":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"},"content":{"kind":"markdown","value":"# The vocabulary of a Twilite graph\n\nSeven structural roles stay small and precise. **Relationships do the composition.**\n\nThe graph below uses the Topic class system to document those primitives, and its own edges use the semantic ports defined by the evolved Topic classes."}}},{"id":"gp-topic","type":"topic","label":"Topic","position":{"x":-80,"y":-160},"width":480,"height":380,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"concepts","label":"Concepts","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180},{"id":"current-synthesis","label":"Current Synthesis","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":0}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Twilite's Graph Primitives","description":"The small graph-native vocabulary Twilite uses to construct durable artifacts, interfaces, navigation, presentation, and authority boundaries.","scope":"Declaration, Port, View, Content, Glyph, Portal, Bridge, plus the relationship semantics that compose these primitives.","overview":"Twilite does not make every application concern into a new primitive. Instead, each primitive owns a narrow contract and relationships assign roles between them. This keeps the semantic graph independent from any one renderer, layout, storage boundary, or application workflow.","keyQuestions":"What does each primitive own? What belongs on an edge instead of a node? How do semantic ports remain independent of visual handles? How can graphs compose across boundaries without losing identity or authority?","documentation":"This graph is also a worked example of the Topic template. Its Topic, Concept, Claim, Mechanism, Perspective, Source, and Synthesis nodes use the v0.3 additive documentation fields and semantic ports.","definitionKey":"topic","_classBinding":{"key":"topic","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-c-decl","type":"topic-concept","label":"Concept","position":{"x":-1000,"y":220},"width":440,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":345},{"id":"claims","label":"Claims","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":15}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Declaration","definition":"The artifact-level contract that gives a graph durable identity, intent, authority, dependencies, and exposed surfaces.","documentation":"A Declaration is the semantic anchor for a graph-backed artifact. It tells consumers what the artifact is and which surfaces or interfaces it intentionally exposes. It also provides the implicit root interface, so a graph has a stable semantic entry point without inventing a separate root node merely for plumbing.","examples":"A documentation graph declares a default detail surface, summary surface, icon surface, glyph, and landing surface. A node-class graph declares the class key, shared presentation surfaces, editor surface, defaults, and class contract.","notes":"Declaration relationships such as default-view, shared-summary, shared-icon, shared-glyph, and landing-surface assign semantic roles to other primitives. Those roles are relationships, not properties intrinsic to the target View.","definitionKey":"topic-concept","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-concept","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-c-port","type":"topic-concept","label":"Concept","position":{"x":-500,"y":220},"width":440,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":345},{"id":"claims","label":"Claims","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":15}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Port","definition":"A named semantic endpoint exposed by a node or graph.","documentation":"Ports describe what may connect and what that connection means. They are part of the semantic contract. A Port can expose a capability, a relationship role, a value path, a navigation target, or another interface. Its identity must survive changes in layout and rendering.","examples":"The v0.3 Topic class exposes `concepts` and `current-synthesis` ports. The Concept class exposes `mechanism` and `claims`. These names make the relationships in this graph readable without deriving meaning from screen position.","notes":"A Port is not a Handle. A Handle is a visual anchor for a Port. Moving a Handle must not rename or redefine the semantic endpoint.","definitionKey":"topic-concept","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-concept","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-c-view","type":"topic-concept","label":"Concept","position":{"x":0,"y":220},"width":440,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":345},{"id":"claims","label":"Claims","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":15}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"View","definition":"A renderable projection of semantic graph data.","documentation":"A View owns a presentation contract, not the underlying semantic object. The same node or graph may expose multiple Views for different semantic levels, contexts, or renderers. A View becomes a detail, summary, icon, editor, or document surface because of its authored relationship and payload contract.","examples":"The Topic classes expose `node.web.detail`, `node.web.summary`, `node.web.icon`, and `editor.web` Views. Semantic zoom can select among them without changing the identity of the underlying node.","notes":"Do not encode semantic role only in labels such as 'Summary View'. The relationship from the Declaration or Port is authoritative.","definitionKey":"topic-concept","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-concept","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-c-content","type":"topic-concept","label":"Concept","position":{"x":500,"y":220},"width":440,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":345},{"id":"claims","label":"Claims","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":15}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Content","definition":"Durable renderable material consumed by a View.","documentation":"Content separates authored material from the surface that presents it. A Content node can hold markdown, HTML, SVG, text, JSON, images, or other supported forms while a View determines how and where that material is presented.","examples":"A landing surface can point to markdown Content. A visual explanation can point to SVG Content. Multiple Views can project the same semantic object while delegating durable prose or graphics to Content nodes.","notes":"Content owns material. View owns presentation. Keeping those contracts separate prevents presentation structure from becoming the authoritative semantic record.","definitionKey":"topic-concept","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-concept","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-c-glyph","type":"topic-concept","label":"Concept","position":{"x":-750,"y":600},"width":440,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":345},{"id":"claims","label":"Claims","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":15}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Glyph","definition":"A compact symbolic identity that can be reused independently of a larger icon View.","documentation":"A Glyph provides the smallest symbolic representation of an artifact or semantic object. It is intentionally narrower than an icon View: the Glyph says 'which symbol identifies this thing', while a View decides how that symbol participates in a rendered surface.","examples":"This graph uses the Material `Hub` glyph for its graph-primitives identity. A future 3D or VR renderer can reuse the same glyph identity without reusing the 2D icon card.","notes":"Keeping Glyph separate from View lets symbolic identity survive changes in renderer and semantic zoom.","definitionKey":"topic-concept","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-concept","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-c-portal","type":"topic-concept","label":"Concept","position":{"x":-250,"y":600},"width":440,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":345},{"id":"claims","label":"Claims","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":15}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Portal","definition":"A consumer-side opening through which another graph interface can be entered or expanded.","documentation":"A Portal composes graphs without pretending that a remote graph has become local data. It preserves the addressable boundary and expresses how a consumer enters another graph surface or interface. Navigation and inline expansion are behaviors of that relationship, not copies of the remote artifact.","examples":"A Portal can navigate to another graph, or expand a branch into a multi-graph workspace while the remote graph keeps its own declaration, storage, and identity.","notes":"The Portal is not the remote View, Port, or graph. It is the local consumer-side opening to that external interface.","definitionKey":"topic-concept","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-concept","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-c-bridge","type":"topic-concept","label":"Concept","position":{"x":250,"y":600},"width":440,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":270},{"id":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":345},{"id":"claims","label":"Claims","direction":"output","dataType":"any","angle":15}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Bridge","definition":"An explicit cross-graph participation or authority boundary.","documentation":"A Bridge makes external authority visible in the graph. It can import a node class, connect reciprocal graph contributions, or represent a participant whose authoritative semantic content lives elsewhere. This prevents hidden runtime magic from masquerading as local ownership.","examples":"Every Topic semantic node in this graph is backed by a node-class Bridge granting read/create/update authority. Fragment extraction also converts the host participant into a Bridge that shares the semantic nodeId with the fragment authority.","notes":"A type string alone does not grant external behavior or class authority. The Bridge makes that dependency explicit and addressable.","definitionKey":"topic-concept","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-concept","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-concept.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-claim","type":"topic-claim","label":"Claim","position":{"x":800,"y":600},"width":480,"height":380,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"Concept","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180},{"id":"perspective","label":"Perspective","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":0},{"id":"source","label":"Source","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":90}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Semantic role lives in relationships","claim":"A primitive's semantic role in a graph should be established by explicit relationships and endpoint contracts rather than inferred from labels, geometry, or duplicated presentation metadata.","rationale":"Keeping role on relationships allows the same narrow primitive to participate in many compositions without changing its intrinsic identity. It also lets renderers, layouts, and storage boundaries change without silently changing graph meaning.","evidence":"In this graph, Topic-to-Concept edges originate from the Topic's `concepts` port; Concept-to-Mechanism edges originate from `mechanism`; Concept-to-Claim edges originate from `claims`; and Claim relationships expose explicit `perspective` and `source` endpoints. The graph remains readable even if every node is moved.","caveats":"Some role information still exists in node-class defaults and View payloads because endpoints need typed contracts and renderers need selectable surfaces. The rule is not 'edges contain everything'; it is 'do not infer semantics from incidental presentation or geometry when an explicit contract can carry them.'","documentation":"This claim is the organizing design rule behind the primitive set. It is what allows a small vocabulary to scale into richer graph-backed artifacts.","confidence":"high","definitionKey":"topic-claim","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-claim","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-claim.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-mech","type":"topic-mechanism","label":"Mechanism","position":{"x":-420,"y":1020},"width":540,"height":400,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Relationships compose the vocabulary","mechanism":"Twilite builds richer artifacts by connecting narrow primitives through explicit semantic roles. Declarations expose surfaces and interfaces; Ports name semantic endpoints; Views project presentation; Content supplies durable material; Glyphs supply compact identity; Portals consume external interfaces; Bridges make cross-graph participation and authority explicit.","conditions":"Semantic endpoints must be authored explicitly. Required singular relationships must remain singular. Port identity must not be inferred from Handle placement. External class or graph authority must be represented by a Bridge or equivalent declared contract.","steps":"1. A Declaration establishes the graph or class identity and exposed surfaces.\n2. Ports name additional semantic endpoints.\n3. Relationships connect those endpoints to Views, Content, Glyphs, Portals, Bridges, or other semantic nodes.\n4. Handles project Port geometry for the active renderer without redefining Port meaning.\n5. Consumers resolve the relationships to choose presentation, navigation, authority, and behavior.\n6. New renderers or workspaces can reinterpret presentation while preserving the same semantic contracts.","failureModes":"Coherence breaks when geometry is treated as semantics, when a label substitutes for an authored relationship, when class authority is inferred from a type string, when remote graph content is silently flattened into local ownership, or when multiple singular-role relationships compete without an explicit rule.","documentation":"The mechanism is compositional rather than hierarchical. No single primitive needs to know every possible application. The system stays extensible because new capabilities can usually be expressed as new relationships, behaviors, Views, or classes before a new core primitive is justified.","definitionKey":"topic-mechanism","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-mechanism","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-mechanism.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-persp","type":"topic-perspective","label":"Perspective","position":{"x":180,"y":1020},"width":540,"height":380,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Small vocabulary, large compositional space","perspective":"Twilite treats the graph as the durable object and keeps the structural vocabulary intentionally small. Specialization is pushed into relationships, node classes, content kinds, behaviors, graph boundaries, and renderer-specific projections.","distinction":"An application-first graph system usually invents node types around one workflow and treats the graph as a UI or orchestration feature. Twilite instead tries to make the graph model general enough that many applications can be projections of the same semantic artifact.","implications":"A stable primitive set reduces migration pressure and makes new capabilities easier to compose. Multi-graph workspaces, fragments, semantic zoom, dynamic ports, and future spatial renderers can extend the system without redefining what a Declaration, Port, or View fundamentally is.","documentation":"This perspective is a design preference, not a claim that the primitive set can never grow. A new primitive is justified when a genuinely distinct semantic responsibility cannot be represented cleanly by existing primitives and relationships.","definitionKey":"topic-perspective","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-perspective","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-perspective.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-source","type":"topic-source","label":"Source","position":{"x":-420,"y":1460},"width":540,"height":360,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Graph-Native Node Composition Contract","citation":"github://twilite-zone/public/library/documentation/contracts/nodes/graph-native-node-composition.md","contribution":"Defines the current write-new authority for graph-native composition, including Declaration, Port, View, Content, Glyph, Portal, relationship roles, and interface geometry.","provenance":"Twilite public documentation contract. The Topic graph summarizes and interprets this contract rather than replacing it as normative authority.","documentation":"Use the source contract when authoring or validating graph-native structure. This Topic graph is intended as an explanatory companion that makes the model easier to explore.","notes":"Where explanatory prose here and the current contract disagree, the current write-new contract should be treated as authoritative.","definitionKey":"topic-source","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-source","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-source.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-synth","type":"topic-synthesis","label":"Synthesis","position":{"x":180,"y":1460},"width":620,"height":400,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180}],"visible":true,"showLabel":false,"data":{"title":"Primitives stay small; graphs become expressive","summary":"Declarations establish artifact identity and exposed surfaces. Ports define named semantic endpoints. Views project presentation. Content owns durable material. Glyphs provide compact symbolic identity. Portals consume external graph interfaces. Bridges make authority and cross-graph participation explicit. Relationships compose those responsibilities into richer artifacts without overloading any one primitive.","implications":"Because semantic identity, endpoints, presentation, content, navigation, authority, and geometry are separate contracts, Twilite can add new Views, workspaces, renderers, fragments, and class capabilities while preserving the meaning of existing graphs. The graph can grow without requiring every old instance to be rewritten.","openQuestions":"Which future capabilities truly require a new primitive, and which belong as relationships, behaviors, Views, or node classes? How should dynamic ports and derived instances advertise their semantics while preserving stable identity? Which contracts are needed before the same model projects naturally into 3D and VR?","documentation":"This synthesis reflects the current model rather than freezing it. The v0.3 Topic classes themselves demonstrate additive evolution: old instances can keep their semantic data while inheriting richer class surfaces, ports, and authoring capabilities.","definitionKey":"topic-synthesis","_classBinding":{"key":"topic-synthesis","sourceRef":"github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic-synthesis.node-class.node"},"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"}}},{"id":"gp-b-topic","type":"bridge","label":"Topic Class Authority","position":{"x":-1400,"y":-180},"width":340,"height":160,"ports":[{"id":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":210}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"twilite-graph-primitives"},"bridge":{"ref"